00:10:54.501 --> 00:10:55.334
(laughter)

00:10:55.334 --> 00:10:57.702
- Advantages, the women.

00:10:57.702 --> 00:10:59.590
(laughter)

00:10:59.590 --> 00:11:01.082
No, no. (laughs)

00:11:01.082 --> 00:11:01.915
- Just wanna hear

00:11:01.915 --> 00:11:03.328
what people say.
- No, no, just kidding.

00:11:03.328 --> 00:11:04.220
- No, you're not kidding.

00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:05.498
You are definitely not kidding.

00:11:05.498 --> 00:11:07.863
- No. (laughs)

00:11:07.863 --> 00:11:08.696
- That may promise everyone.

00:11:08.696 --> 00:11:12.023
- No, I mean, you can, (laughs)

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:16.240
I mean, we are quite like,
I'm a public person, right?

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:18.760
People already know us from
playing the Premier League,

00:11:18.760 --> 00:11:21.420
and they see us on TV or whatever.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:25.810
And some people, they judge
you by the way you look

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:26.643
or whatever.
- Sure.

00:11:26.643 --> 00:11:29.230
- And what I like about
social media is that

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.100
you can actually show
yourself on a different side,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:34.680
other interests you have, maybe
fashion or whatever, right?

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.680
And also, you can still control
what you want people to see,

00:11:39.839 --> 00:11:43.280
because I would not necessarily put

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:46.326
me with my family or whatever in that

00:11:46.326 --> 00:11:49.149
because at the end of the
day, it's social media.

00:11:49.149 --> 00:11:50.377
- It's, you're in control, right?

00:11:50.377 --> 00:11:51.717
You're in control about

00:11:51.717 --> 00:11:54.450
painting a more complete
picture about yourself.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:57.870
You're in control that it's not
somebody commentating on you

00:11:57.870 --> 00:12:00.788
and your play and your social
life, it's you projecting it.

00:12:00.788 --> 00:12:03.680
What platforms do you use?

00:12:03.680 --> 00:12:07.100
- I use Instagram, I use
Facebook, I use Twitter.

00:12:07.100 --> 00:12:08.688
- And how do you use them differently?

00:12:08.688 --> 00:12:09.970
- Well, so I--

00:12:09.970 --> 00:12:11.360
- What do you use Facebook for?

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:12.448
That's interesting to me.

00:12:12.448 --> 00:12:13.860
- To be honest with you, Facebook,

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:15.068
I used to have a private account,

00:12:15.068 --> 00:12:17.940
and now I have people
running it for me, like--

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:19.499
- Your fan page.
- Like a fan page.

00:12:19.499 --> 00:12:23.183
But me, I just started to have
a bit more fun on Instagram

00:12:23.183 --> 00:12:26.184
because I think it's just
newer and it's more--

00:12:26.184 --> 00:12:28.061
- Correct.
- Yeah, and more

00:12:28.061 --> 00:12:30.270
for the younger generation, so--

00:12:30.270 --> 00:12:31.540
- And what about Twitter?

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:34.918
- Twitter, I have a team
who's running it for me too.

00:12:34.918 --> 00:12:36.667
Basically what I put on my Instagram,

00:12:36.667 --> 00:12:39.890
it's sent out to what I put on my Twitter.

00:12:39.890 --> 00:12:42.160
- Do you engage ever, or
are you just consuming?

00:12:42.160 --> 00:12:44.216
- It's just consuming, I would say.

00:12:44.216 --> 00:12:45.783
'Cause at the end of the day,

00:12:46.995 --> 00:12:50.150
what I don't like is when we lose a game

00:12:50.150 --> 00:12:51.058
on a Saturday--
- And people

00:12:51.058 --> 00:12:51.891
tell you you suck.

00:12:51.891 --> 00:12:54.410
- And people tell you, that's
cool running into that.

00:12:54.410 --> 00:12:57.830
But I feel if you lose,
and you post a picture

00:12:57.830 --> 00:13:00.990
two minutes after about you enjoying,

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:01.823
I don't know--
- A meal.

00:13:01.823 --> 00:13:02.773
- A meal or whatever,

00:13:02.773 --> 00:13:03.606
they will say--
- They're like why don't you

00:13:03.606 --> 00:13:04.690
fucking focus on the game?
- Why don't you fucking focus

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:05.721
on the game?

00:13:05.721 --> 00:13:08.280
And I'm like Jesus
fucking Christ. (laughs)

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:10.500
I'm a human being, so
I need to live as well.

00:13:10.500 --> 00:13:13.031
- Uh-huh, fair enough, I get it.

00:13:13.031 --> 00:13:15.281
(laughter)

00:13:16.281 --> 00:13:19.460
Talk to me about, just
because it's so current,

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:21.480
obviously the US national
team wasn't involved,

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:24.162
but how did you process the World Cup?

00:13:24.162 --> 00:13:25.330
Did you go?

00:13:25.330 --> 00:13:26.410
Who were you rooting for?

00:13:26.410 --> 00:13:27.720
How did you think about it?

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.667
Obviously Germany under performed.

00:13:30.667 --> 00:13:33.533
England performed extremely well.

00:13:35.719 --> 00:13:37.957
- [Audience] Boo.

00:13:37.957 --> 00:13:39.818
(laughter)

00:13:39.818 --> 00:13:41.926
- How did you, did you go?

00:13:41.926 --> 00:13:43.450
- No, I didn't go.
- Okay, did you want to go,

00:13:43.450 --> 00:13:44.478
or you just couldn't go?

00:13:44.478 --> 00:13:45.311
- No, I did not--

00:13:45.311 --> 00:13:46.240
- You had nowhere to fight,

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:47.780
'cause you were salty about
not being in it, right?

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:50.660
- No, because I broke my
ankle four months ago,

00:13:50.660 --> 00:13:52.260
and I was focusing on my recovery--

00:13:52.260 --> 00:13:55.730
- Respect. Who'd you think
was gonna win, for real?

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:56.920
Who did you think was gonna win?

00:13:56.920 --> 00:14:00.160
- If I had to place a bet,
it would be Spain or Brazil.

00:14:00.160 --> 00:14:01.960
- Right, and so how did
you think it played out?

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:03.920
What caught your attention most

00:14:03.920 --> 00:14:05.360
about this year's tournament?

00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:07.308
- I think the team that
surprised me the most

00:14:07.308 --> 00:14:08.407
was definitely Croatia.
- Of course.

00:14:08.407 --> 00:14:11.490
- Because they have
amazing technical players

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:13.380
but when you see the size of the country--

00:14:13.380 --> 00:14:14.500
- It's just no room, yeah.

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:17.370
- Exactly, so that they came that far,

00:14:17.370 --> 00:14:19.630
it's like big respect to them.

00:14:19.630 --> 00:14:22.980
- Yep, so what about
technology as a whole?

00:14:22.980 --> 00:14:24.080
And then I'm gonna open it up.

00:14:24.080 --> 00:14:26.520
So for the questions for
everyone in the audience,

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.930
you wanna get very nerdy
about proper football,

00:14:28.930 --> 00:14:29.990
do your thing, enjoy it.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:32.430
If you wanna ask me a question
about business stuff, fine,

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.410
wherever you wanna go, we're here for you.

00:14:35.410 --> 00:14:38.290
Before we open it up to the questions,

00:14:38.290 --> 00:14:41.649
technology as a whole,
having an Alexa at your home,

00:14:41.649 --> 00:14:45.660
the way you watch TV,
how much TV do you watch?

00:14:45.660 --> 00:14:47.500
- To be honest with you,
I don't watch a lot of TV.

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:50.040
- So when you do watch TV, is it sports?

00:14:50.040 --> 00:14:53.710
Is it movies, is it
Netflix, how do you roll?

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.070
- Most of the time, it's Netflix if--

00:14:55.070 --> 00:14:56.483
- And what are you watching?

00:14:57.550 --> 00:15:01.673
- I watch 13 Reasons Why
for the moment. (laughs)

00:15:02.740 --> 00:15:04.050
- You and a lot of other people.

00:15:04.050 --> 00:15:09.050
- Yeah, and then some people
have me watching Love Island.

00:15:09.224 --> 00:15:12.043
(laughter)

00:15:12.043 --> 00:15:14.770
- Guilty pleasures always do well.

00:15:14.770 --> 00:15:15.960
What about gaming?

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:17.470
Do you play mobile games?

00:15:17.470 --> 00:15:18.733
Do you play FIFA?

00:15:18.733 --> 00:15:20.330
Do you play Fortnite?

00:15:20.330 --> 00:15:21.920
Are you doing anything in gaming?

00:15:21.920 --> 00:15:23.007
- I used to play FIFA a lot,

00:15:23.007 --> 00:15:26.000
and I used to play Pro Evolution Soccer.

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:30.210
But I have to say that that
doesn't interest me no more.

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.563
Because, I don't know, as footballers,

00:15:32.563 --> 00:15:34.342
we have this weird life.

00:15:34.342 --> 00:15:38.260
We go to practice every morning,
and people think we don't

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.650
do anything but we
actually do, stretching.

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:44.310
And then we come home,
and I don't have the--

00:15:44.310 --> 00:15:45.810
- Energy.
- The energy to

00:15:45.810 --> 00:15:48.310
actually go and sit down
and game or whatever.

00:15:48.310 --> 00:15:51.270
I just like to chill, watch Netflix,

00:15:51.270 --> 00:15:53.670
or talk to friends or meet with friends

00:15:53.670 --> 00:15:55.665
and actually connect--
- Live.

00:15:55.665 --> 00:15:58.105
- With them, instead of social media,

00:15:58.105 --> 00:16:00.215
check WhatsApp, how are you, whatever.

00:16:00.215 --> 00:16:03.243
I just like to network
with people in real life,

00:16:03.243 --> 00:16:05.925
I think that's what we're supposed to do

00:16:05.925 --> 00:16:08.317
still as well, so I like that.

00:16:08.317 --> 00:16:09.980
- How do you actually, I apologize,

00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:11.480
one more question before I open it up.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:15.610
How do you think about
your post athletic career?

00:16:15.610 --> 00:16:20.560
What are your ambitions when
you're done with playing?

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:24.400
- Well, I started to invest
in some properties when I was

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:27.610
coming here, so I own four
properties now in London.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:32.610
And I was actually having a
meeting with one of my people,

00:16:34.053 --> 00:16:37.210
the companies who work for
me, and we went through it,

00:16:37.210 --> 00:16:41.092
and obviously one of these
questions was actually,

00:16:41.092 --> 00:16:42.260
what are you gonna do after football?

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:45.293
And I think what I wanna focus more on is

00:16:45.293 --> 00:16:48.920
rehab centers because
I was quite unfortunate

00:16:48.920 --> 00:16:51.410
through my career with injuries,

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:56.410
and I think there is so much
more space in the development,

00:16:56.717 --> 00:16:59.200
the treatment, especially in the UK,

00:16:59.200 --> 00:17:03.390
to improve rehabilitation, so that's one--

00:17:03.390 --> 00:17:06.045
- Where do you think about living?

00:17:06.045 --> 00:17:07.070
Have you thought about that?

00:17:07.070 --> 00:17:09.933
- I love Los Angeles, I really love it.

00:17:09.933 --> 00:17:12.050
I do like it a lot, but (laughs)

00:17:12.050 --> 00:17:14.840
London, definitely,
because I used to live here

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:17.653
for four years and it's close to home.

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:20.290
'Cause my parents are,
obviously they live in Germany,

00:17:20.290 --> 00:17:22.925
it's just like one and
a half hour flight, so--

00:17:22.925 --> 00:17:24.690
- You're thinking about
splitting your time there.

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:26.250
- Exactly.
- I love it.

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:27.613
Great, who's got questions?

00:17:29.820 --> 00:17:30.980
- [Audience Member] I
just wanted to ask you,

00:17:30.980 --> 00:17:33.203
when you went to probably the academy

00:17:33.203 --> 00:17:34.690
into the German national team,

00:17:34.690 --> 00:17:36.444
how do you find the
quirk when you're playing

00:17:36.444 --> 00:17:37.617
a lot better players and stuff?

00:17:37.617 --> 00:17:39.670
Did you have it affect your confidence?

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:42.380
- Yes, like massively,
it was quite intimidating

00:17:42.380 --> 00:17:44.240
because you are in the club,

00:17:44.240 --> 00:17:47.330
and they say to you that you're
the biggest talent, right?

00:17:47.330 --> 00:17:49.880
So you think, okay, I'm gonna go there,

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:51.180
and I'm gonna rip it up.

00:17:51.180 --> 00:17:53.090
But then you actually
play with some players

00:17:53.090 --> 00:17:56.640
from different counties, different areas

00:17:56.640 --> 00:17:58.974
from the same country,
and it's like, okay,

00:17:58.974 --> 00:18:01.311
you have actually players
that are better than you,

00:18:01.311 --> 00:18:03.280
and then you start to realize, okay,

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:05.150
maybe I'm not as good or whatever, so--

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:07.810
- Who was the first player
you ever came across,

00:18:07.810 --> 00:18:09.590
even if it was when you were five,

00:18:09.590 --> 00:18:13.840
or when it was this big jump
where you were like, holy shit?

00:18:13.840 --> 00:18:15.750
Even if it's somebody famous
or if it's some person

00:18:15.750 --> 00:18:18.300
that never made it, do you
recall the first time you came

00:18:18.300 --> 00:18:20.830
across a player that you were
just like, this is different?

00:18:20.830 --> 00:18:23.663
- Two players that stood
out, it was Toni Kroos.

00:18:23.663 --> 00:18:25.163
- I'm sorry?
- Toni Kroos.

00:18:25.163 --> 00:18:27.890
When I played against him in the youth,

00:18:27.890 --> 00:18:30.207
I was like, this kid is
something else, and--

00:18:30.207 --> 00:18:31.760
- 'Cause of the speed?

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:32.920
- No, not because of the speed,

00:18:32.920 --> 00:18:36.047
because I think I'm
quicker than him. (laughs)

00:18:36.047 --> 00:18:37.990
And just the way he moves the ball

00:18:37.990 --> 00:18:39.430
and it's fluctuating--
- The fluid nature.

00:18:39.430 --> 00:18:42.570
- It's just crazy and the
talent, the raw talent he has,

00:18:42.570 --> 00:18:44.273
and also his ability to.

00:18:45.610 --> 00:18:48.337
Some people probably
don't like me to say that,

00:18:48.337 --> 00:18:50.013
but he gets a lot of
steak, which I understand

00:18:50.013 --> 00:18:53.842
because of his body language
and the way he carries himself.

00:18:53.842 --> 00:18:56.070
- What stood out to you when
you first crossed his path?

00:18:56.070 --> 00:18:58.106
- With him, it was just his calmness.

00:18:58.106 --> 00:19:02.423
I think I played against
him when he was probably 18,

00:19:03.282 --> 00:19:08.279
18, 19, and I think he used to
play for Bayern at the time.

00:19:08.279 --> 00:19:11.939
And he was just like, how he
moved the ball and everything,

00:19:11.939 --> 00:19:15.380
he actually took the
game in his hands already

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:18.083
and he was 19, and everybody
who ever played football

00:19:18.083 --> 00:19:20.810
will know how tough it
is to control the game,

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:22.460
especially when you're a young player.

00:19:22.460 --> 00:19:25.120
Because I mean, you
have to have confidence,

00:19:25.120 --> 00:19:26.910
that you have other
people to believe in you

00:19:26.910 --> 00:19:27.977
to get you the ball and stuff.

00:19:27.977 --> 00:19:31.530
And the way he created
the chances and has,

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.680
I think he's always two,
three steps ahead of you,

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.010
he knows situations--
- The game's playing

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:37.490
out up here.
- Exactly.

00:19:37.490 --> 00:19:39.810
That's like the biggest surprise for me.

00:19:39.810 --> 00:19:41.860
- Very cool, questions?

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:42.693
Yes, sir.

00:19:42.693 --> 00:19:45.320
- [Audience Member] What was
the key thing to overcome

00:19:46.256 --> 00:19:50.427
to get around which are
pessimistic when you were working?

00:19:51.786 --> 00:19:54.730
- The question is how
were you able to overcome

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:59.404
the inevitable pessimistic cynicism?

00:19:59.404 --> 00:20:03.920
At some point, whether you
were 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14,

00:20:03.920 --> 00:20:05.800
you're saying I'm gonna be
a professional football,

00:20:05.800 --> 00:20:07.940
the odds of that are quite low.

00:20:07.940 --> 00:20:12.010
How were you able to not hear
your friend or your uncle

00:20:12.010 --> 00:20:13.720
or a neighbor, I think for a lot of us,

00:20:13.720 --> 00:20:15.810
I think it maps to everything in life,

00:20:15.810 --> 00:20:18.070
how were you able to shut out the noise?

00:20:18.070 --> 00:20:23.020
What was it inside of you,
parenting, results, somebody,

00:20:23.020 --> 00:20:26.530
why were you able to always,
'cause there's no way

00:20:26.530 --> 00:20:30.210
you end up to where you are
without, at every insecurity,

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:33.770
be able to say no, I'm gonna
get over this hump, versus

00:20:33.770 --> 00:20:35.890
I'm gonna go this way, or
I can't get over the hump,

00:20:35.890 --> 00:20:37.320
or let me go backwards.

00:20:37.320 --> 00:20:40.080
- For me, it was quite easy I think.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.790
Whenever I watched Carter
McGraddon's documentary,

00:20:42.790 --> 00:20:45.090
you know he always speaks
about visualizaton?

00:20:45.090 --> 00:20:45.923
- Yes.

00:20:45.923 --> 00:20:48.960
- And I have to admit, the minute I was,

00:20:48.960 --> 00:20:51.520
I had the bottom line on my feet,

00:20:51.520 --> 00:20:55.030
I knew, I just visualized it
in my head that I was like,

00:20:55.030 --> 00:20:57.927
I'm about to be a
professional football player,

00:20:57.927 --> 00:21:01.287
and it sounds weird but
I always had this belief

00:21:01.287 --> 00:21:03.970
and I saw myself kicking
the ball in the stadium,

00:21:03.970 --> 00:21:06.726
and it was crazy when
I played my first game,

00:21:06.726 --> 00:21:10.080
and my parents, they stood there crying,

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:14.833
I visualized that, about, I
don't know, 17 years earlier,

00:21:14.833 --> 00:21:15.666
you know what I mean?

00:21:15.666 --> 00:21:17.000
- Did your parents look the way

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.055
you visualized them 17 years earlier?

00:21:19.055 --> 00:21:20.363
(laughter)

00:21:20.363 --> 00:21:21.196
- Well, I don't know.

00:21:21.196 --> 00:21:22.285
(laughter)

00:21:22.285 --> 00:21:23.118
No, but--
- I actually,

00:21:23.118 --> 00:21:24.130
you know why I'm asking you that?

00:21:24.130 --> 00:21:26.927
I would argue I do a
lot of similar things,

00:21:26.927 --> 00:21:30.040
and they're just premonitions,
it's not very clear.

00:21:30.040 --> 00:21:33.730
But I always laugh that
the people at the time

00:21:33.730 --> 00:21:35.320
look the way they look then,

00:21:35.320 --> 00:21:37.670
and I always think it's
funny 'cause 20 years later,

00:21:37.670 --> 00:21:39.900
they're much older and it looks different.

00:21:39.900 --> 00:21:41.664
It looks different than
what it was in your mind.

00:21:41.664 --> 00:21:42.730
- No, definitely.
- I get that.

00:21:42.730 --> 00:21:45.574
- And also, like I just said,

00:21:45.574 --> 00:21:47.100
when I grew up--
- Were your parents

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:48.830
blindly on board?

00:21:48.830 --> 00:21:50.298
Would your mom--
- No, no, no.

00:21:50.298 --> 00:21:54.137
I had a lot of trouble when I grew up.

00:21:54.137 --> 00:21:55.987
When I moved to this academy, my mom,

00:21:56.859 --> 00:21:58.709
she was diagnosed with breast cancer,

00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:03.120
and my dad had a lot of problems.

00:22:03.120 --> 00:22:05.050
My brother, he was in a lot of trouble,

00:22:05.050 --> 00:22:09.040
so I ran from this, not the easiest,

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:10.330
but I always said to myself,

00:22:10.330 --> 00:22:12.830
I wanna be the one who
stands up in the family

00:22:12.830 --> 00:22:15.110
and wanna make them proud
and I wanna make myself proud

00:22:15.110 --> 00:22:15.950
because end of the day,

00:22:15.950 --> 00:22:18.564
I only have to prove that stuff to myself.

00:22:18.564 --> 00:22:20.680
- When you're going through
those kind of difficulties

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:23.470
at such a young age, was the pitch,

00:22:23.470 --> 00:22:26.820
was the field a escape from all the shit?

00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:29.070
- Definitely, like I was--
- It was the medicine?

00:22:29.070 --> 00:22:31.671
- Yeah, I always felt free
when I was on the pitch.

00:22:31.671 --> 00:22:36.400
All the bullshit, what's going
on in the family, whatever,

00:22:36.400 --> 00:22:38.570
when I, the minute I step
my foot on the pitch--

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:40.010
- It's gone.
- It's gone, I'm free.

00:22:40.010 --> 00:22:42.680
So and then it's funny
because when I had more anger,

00:22:42.680 --> 00:22:45.614
or when my parents,
when they were fighting,

00:22:45.614 --> 00:22:48.450
two, three hours before a game,

00:22:48.450 --> 00:22:51.825
I would play better,
because I was so angry

00:22:51.825 --> 00:22:54.150
and I just let it on the pitch.

00:22:54.150 --> 00:22:55.430
- I understand.

00:22:55.430 --> 00:22:57.192
Questions? Yes, sir.

00:22:57.192 --> 00:22:58.100
- [Audience Member] How'd you find,

00:22:58.100 --> 00:23:00.130
when you were getting closer
and closer to the game,

00:23:00.130 --> 00:23:02.664
when people changing room
and see what goes on,

00:23:02.664 --> 00:23:05.295
24/7 on the clock.

00:23:05.295 --> 00:23:06.963
As players, how do you feel about that?

00:23:06.963 --> 00:23:09.030
- I would repeat that.

00:23:09.030 --> 00:23:10.440
It's a great question, thank you for that.

00:23:10.440 --> 00:23:13.120
The question is, as fans, we are now,

00:23:13.120 --> 00:23:14.889
especially in the new digital age,

00:23:14.889 --> 00:23:18.510
yearning for, aspiring,
hoping that it could be

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:21.790
a 24/7 thing in the locker
room after the match.

00:23:21.790 --> 00:23:23.680
We wanna watch you eat
dinner after a match

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:24.665
and what you're talking about.

00:23:24.665 --> 00:23:27.110
We want that access.

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:29.743
As a player, how do you feel about that?

00:23:30.610 --> 00:23:32.490
- To be honest with you,
I have nothing against it

00:23:32.490 --> 00:23:34.910
because I love going in the dressing room

00:23:34.910 --> 00:23:37.280
and having banter with the boys,

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:39.980
and it's just like so
different from the outer world

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:43.450
because even with some friends that I have

00:23:43.450 --> 00:23:46.800
and they don't have to do
anything with football,

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:49.350
they're just so different, like the,

00:23:49.350 --> 00:23:51.160
I don't know, the nature
of jokes and stuff,

00:23:51.160 --> 00:23:52.220
they laugh about different things.

00:23:52.220 --> 00:23:54.850
They laugh about football
as well and laugh,

00:23:54.850 --> 00:23:58.470
so it's so different and I
don't have nothing against it.

00:23:58.470 --> 00:23:59.850
I'll engage it.

00:23:59.850 --> 00:24:00.900
- Let me ask you a question

00:24:00.900 --> 00:24:02.290
that I've always been interested in.

00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.420
Have there been in the
last three, four years,

00:24:04.420 --> 00:24:08.230
as this world has exploded,
this always on content world,

00:24:08.230 --> 00:24:10.570
have there been teams
that you've been a part of

00:24:10.570 --> 00:24:14.480
that some of the players look
down on the other players

00:24:14.480 --> 00:24:16.930
that are spending a lot of time
trying to build their brand

00:24:16.930 --> 00:24:19.430
on Instagram, trying to
sign entrepreneurship?

00:24:19.430 --> 00:24:21.020
Is there an animosity of like,

00:24:21.020 --> 00:24:23.063
this isn't the way we should be doing it?

00:24:25.350 --> 00:24:28.700
You talk about the fans saying,
hey, why are you eating?

00:24:28.700 --> 00:24:32.264
Is there a level of tension
in the locker rooms of

00:24:32.264 --> 00:24:35.070
some guys doing three public appearances

00:24:35.070 --> 00:24:37.990
and had had a keynote at a tech conference

00:24:37.990 --> 00:24:39.680
and isn't playing as well as

00:24:39.680 --> 00:24:42.030
the team mate thinks that he should?

00:24:42.030 --> 00:24:43.840
Is there a little bit of that old school,

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:45.750
new school tension in the locker room?

00:24:45.750 --> 00:24:48.619
- Yeah, definitely, I mean,
you can definitely sense

00:24:48.619 --> 00:24:52.001
the difference when it comes
to the older generation,

00:24:52.001 --> 00:24:56.020
the players who retire or about to retire,

00:24:56.020 --> 00:24:57.820
the 35, 36 year old.

00:24:57.820 --> 00:25:00.337
Then you have a player who's 18, 19,

00:25:00.337 --> 00:25:02.600
and he just posts every day,

00:25:02.600 --> 00:25:04.074
he posts stuff about what he's wearing,

00:25:04.074 --> 00:25:05.952
or he's in Selfridges, or
he's doing that or he's,

00:25:05.952 --> 00:25:08.610
it's crazy, or what car
he drives, whatever.

00:25:08.610 --> 00:25:10.203
And then you can see the older guys,

00:25:10.203 --> 00:25:13.330
they say, listen, focus
on football, seriously.

00:25:14.970 --> 00:25:17.661
But that's just how it is, I think.

00:25:17.661 --> 00:25:20.240
In this world that we're
living in at the moment,

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.100
it's a bit run by social media as well,

00:25:22.100 --> 00:25:24.760
and if you use it the right way,

00:25:24.760 --> 00:25:27.679
you can actually capitalize on it as well.

00:25:27.679 --> 00:25:28.512
- Yes, sir.

00:25:28.512 --> 00:25:29.970
- [Audience Member] Yeah,
my question's for you, Gary,

00:25:29.970 --> 00:25:32.457
I was just wondering
whether you had any advice

00:25:32.457 --> 00:25:36.069
for smaller companies when
working with big corporations

00:25:36.069 --> 00:25:40.490
and how to not be sort
of taken advantage of.

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:42.480
- Yeah, I mean, the question,
just to repeat it, is,

00:25:42.480 --> 00:25:44.560
when you're a smaller
company, how do you not

00:25:44.560 --> 00:25:47.952
get taken advantage of
by bigger companies?

00:25:47.952 --> 00:25:51.310
Vayner was a very small
company when I started it.

00:25:51.310 --> 00:25:53.220
And we worked with the
biggest companies in the world

00:25:53.220 --> 00:25:55.730
right away with a little
bit of my reputation.

00:25:55.730 --> 00:25:58.000
I have a very interesting answer to this.

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:01.360
It's two fold, first of all,
when you have your own company,

00:26:01.360 --> 00:26:04.160
and you have clients, you're in control of

00:26:04.160 --> 00:26:06.810
whether you're being
taken advantage of or not.

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:10.380
If you're a client provider,
if you have clients,

00:26:10.380 --> 00:26:12.470
you're more than welcome to
pick up the phone and say,

00:26:12.470 --> 00:26:14.570
I don't wanna work with you anymore.

00:26:14.570 --> 00:26:15.680
And here's the money back,

00:26:15.680 --> 00:26:17.320
or I don't want your money anymore.

00:26:17.320 --> 00:26:19.960
So first of all, every small
business just needs to know

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:22.590
that that's the great
part of having a business.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:25.280
You're also in charge, right?

00:26:25.280 --> 00:26:28.810
Number two, I would argue for
a lot of small businesses,

00:26:28.810 --> 00:26:32.120
the goal is to be taken advantage of.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:35.270
I think about the first three
to four years of VaynerMedia,

00:26:35.270 --> 00:26:37.090
very similar to the way Muhammad Ali

00:26:37.090 --> 00:26:39.170
fought George Foreman in Zaire.

00:26:39.170 --> 00:26:41.970
I think I rope-a-doped the industry.

00:26:41.970 --> 00:26:44.860
I think the industry thought
they were taking advantage

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:48.360
of Vayner, and they were, we
were doing so much more work

00:26:48.360 --> 00:26:49.900
for the money they were paying me,

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.110
versus the companies that were

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.480
much bigger than us when we started.

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:56.640
I also think that's why I let it happen

00:26:56.640 --> 00:26:58.430
because that was the value prop,

00:26:58.430 --> 00:26:59.820
that we didn't have reputation,

00:26:59.820 --> 00:27:03.481
I was a wine shop owner,
now and social media

00:27:03.481 --> 00:27:05.770
was not what it is today.

00:27:05.770 --> 00:27:08.030
When I started Vayner in 2009,

00:27:08.030 --> 00:27:10.960
most of our clients
thought that it was a fad.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:14.530
So I think two things,
one, you're in control,

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:18.460
and two, it's actually
probably the leverage you have.

00:27:18.460 --> 00:27:22.190
And I think too many small
businesses go ideology on this,

00:27:22.190 --> 00:27:23.557
they become ideological about it,

00:27:23.557 --> 00:27:26.020
and like, oh, I'm getting
taken advantage of.

00:27:26.020 --> 00:27:29.150
You're a big boy, it's your
bed, you can sleep in it.

00:27:29.150 --> 00:27:30.850
I think you need to use it as an offense,

00:27:30.850 --> 00:27:32.930
I think it is the number one thing

00:27:32.930 --> 00:27:35.960
that a small tiny business
has over a big business

00:27:35.960 --> 00:27:38.100
that needs to make more profit,

00:27:38.100 --> 00:27:42.100
that needs to be publicly
traded and then hit numbers.

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:45.100
I think every small business
should try to get taken

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:48.023
advantage of, that's how
they'll become a big business.

00:27:49.209 --> 00:27:50.330
(clapping)

00:27:50.330 --> 00:27:51.363
Thanks.

00:27:52.610 --> 00:27:54.223
Yes, sir.

00:27:54.223 --> 00:27:56.720
- [Audience Member] Disregarding catch,

00:27:56.720 --> 00:27:59.711
if you had a chance to transfer
to another Premier League,

00:27:59.711 --> 00:28:01.959
who would it be and why?

00:28:01.959 --> 00:28:03.991
(laughter)

00:28:03.991 --> 00:28:05.718
- Answer it, answer it.

00:28:05.718 --> 00:28:06.724
- You really put me on the spot there.

00:28:06.724 --> 00:28:07.924
- Yes, this is the spot.

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:12.410
- Growing up, I always
applauded the Oslum.

00:28:12.410 --> 00:28:13.243
- [Audience] Yes.

00:28:14.078 --> 00:28:16.328
(laughter)

00:28:17.668 --> 00:28:18.731
You're welcome.

00:28:18.731 --> 00:28:21.590
- That was just because
of the history they have,

00:28:21.590 --> 00:28:26.530
my dream when I was young, I
grew up watching Pablo Pretnia

00:28:26.530 --> 00:28:28.837
and all these players
that went to both TRE,

00:28:29.910 --> 00:28:32.543
and they just create so much history

00:28:32.543 --> 00:28:35.123
that I think if I was
playing for that club,

00:28:35.123 --> 00:28:38.830
I would try everything
to bring the history back

00:28:38.830 --> 00:28:40.970
and also make the fans
proud because I know

00:28:40.970 --> 00:28:43.148
you guys suffer at the
moment with us in the--

00:28:43.148 --> 00:28:45.398
(laughter)

00:28:47.310 --> 00:28:48.633
Yeah--

00:28:48.633 --> 00:28:52.620
- It's an interesting topic,
I become so fascinated,

00:28:52.620 --> 00:28:55.204
so one of the things in
America that I'm spending

00:28:55.204 --> 00:28:58.840
a lot of time making jokes
about on social media is

00:28:58.840 --> 00:29:02.180
if you live in New York City
and you have a Steph Curry

00:29:02.180 --> 00:29:06.379
jersey on that I think you're
a piece of shit, right?

00:29:06.379 --> 00:29:08.800
And to me, it's, I think
you're a piece of shit

00:29:08.800 --> 00:29:10.730
not because you're a
bad person, but because

00:29:10.730 --> 00:29:14.960
I think it's a very interesting psyche of

00:29:14.960 --> 00:29:18.920
when you pick a team,
either based on the history

00:29:18.920 --> 00:29:23.470
or the current winning status
or when you pick an underdog,

00:29:23.470 --> 00:29:26.920
I find it, I'm interested,
I don't understand why,

00:29:26.920 --> 00:29:31.920
why do I hate Kobe Bryant until
the last year of his career

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:34.760
when he's a little more washed
up, then I root for him?

00:29:34.760 --> 00:29:39.080
Why in every situation, if I
turn on any match of any sort,

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:40.340
something I don't even understand,

00:29:40.340 --> 00:29:44.090
if I randomly end up looking,
this actually happened,

00:29:44.090 --> 00:29:46.650
one day I'm clicking through,
my son's getting into sports

00:29:46.650 --> 00:29:47.760
so I'm trying to get him into everything,

00:29:47.760 --> 00:29:50.220
I don't wanna impose my opinions on him,

00:29:50.220 --> 00:29:53.600
I want him to taste things,
and there's a cricket match.

00:29:53.600 --> 00:29:55.030
I can tell you one thing about Americans,

00:29:55.030 --> 00:29:57.070
you don't think they
understand proper football,

00:29:57.070 --> 00:30:00.093
we really don't understand cricket, right?

00:30:00.093 --> 00:30:00.926
- Same.

00:30:00.926 --> 00:30:02.440
- Yeah, I'm like, what the fuck?

00:30:02.440 --> 00:30:04.550
Anyway, you're like hitting a bat,

00:30:04.550 --> 00:30:06.500
so anyway, it's on.

00:30:06.500 --> 00:30:09.330
I literally Google, while the match is on,

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.920
to figure out which team is the favorite,

00:30:11.920 --> 00:30:14.340
and then I spend the next 10 minutes

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:16.880
desperately rooting for the underdog.

00:30:16.880 --> 00:30:21.010
And I'm very interested in,
when I started announcing,

00:30:21.010 --> 00:30:23.480
when we opened this office,
that I wanted to pick a team,

00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:27.010
I'm fascinated that 99% of people are fans

00:30:27.010 --> 00:30:29.130
of the same four to five teams, right,

00:30:29.130 --> 00:30:31.710
we all know who they are,
in the same way in the US

00:30:31.710 --> 00:30:35.250
whether it's the Lakers
or baseball, the Yankees,

00:30:35.250 --> 00:30:39.180
the psyche of people that
wanna jump on bandwagons

00:30:39.180 --> 00:30:40.980
versus the psyche of the people that

00:30:40.980 --> 00:30:43.630
wanna pick something and pick an underdog.

00:30:43.630 --> 00:30:45.020
As a matter of fact, the biggest reason

00:30:45.020 --> 00:30:48.160
I'm not 100% locked into the Spurs is

00:30:48.160 --> 00:30:51.410
my only criteria for picking
my team was that they

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:54.500
couldn't have won the
championship in the last 40 years.

00:30:54.500 --> 00:30:56.630
The problem with the
Spurs is they haven't,

00:30:56.630 --> 00:30:58.340
but they're very fucking boujee.

00:30:58.340 --> 00:31:02.730
And I'm very confused by
it, it doesn't map to my

00:31:02.730 --> 00:31:05.537
Jets and Knicks fandom as
well as I'd like it to.

00:31:05.537 --> 00:31:07.900
So I'm still trying to figure my shit out,

00:31:07.900 --> 00:31:12.100
but yeah, I just basically
think that all of you

00:31:12.100 --> 00:31:15.010
that jump on good teams
when you live in a town

00:31:15.010 --> 00:31:17.363
that has a team, that
you're actually a loser.

00:31:18.470 --> 00:31:19.880
Questions?

00:31:19.880 --> 00:31:20.721
Yes.
- You should support us

00:31:20.721 --> 00:31:22.140
honestly, you should support us actually.

00:31:22.140 --> 00:31:23.350
- Yeah?
- Yeah.

00:31:23.350 --> 00:31:24.183
- No championship

00:31:24.183 --> 00:31:25.016
in 40 years?
- No, no.

00:31:25.016 --> 00:31:26.036
- Alright, I'll support.

00:31:26.036 --> 00:31:27.550
(laughter)

00:31:27.550 --> 00:31:28.383
Yes, Tim?

00:31:28.383 --> 00:31:29.890
- [Tim] A very interesting journey

00:31:30.990 --> 00:31:33.390
from when you grew up
to where you are now,

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.474
was there a particular point when you

00:31:35.474 --> 00:31:37.564
decided to take control of your life?

00:31:37.564 --> 00:31:40.640
Was it something that
just happened at the time?

00:31:40.640 --> 00:31:42.470
- And Tim, before you,
and the question is,

00:31:42.470 --> 00:31:43.540
you've been on this interesting journey

00:31:43.540 --> 00:31:44.790
for a young man 21 years old.

00:31:44.790 --> 00:31:45.780
Was there a tipping point where

00:31:45.780 --> 00:31:47.290
you took control of your life?

00:31:47.290 --> 00:31:49.670
Based on the interview we've
been doing, I'm curious,

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:51.470
are you coming from the perspective

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:54.323
of the adversity he faced
when he was a youngster?

00:31:55.340 --> 00:31:56.470
Add a layer to that question

00:31:56.470 --> 00:31:57.720
'cause I'm curious where you're going.

00:31:57.720 --> 00:31:59.080
- [Tim] He's obviously very,

00:31:59.080 --> 00:32:01.350
you're very savvy about social media.

00:32:01.350 --> 00:32:03.780
Is there a point when you
realized you needed to do that,

00:32:03.780 --> 00:32:05.380
or is it something that--

00:32:05.380 --> 00:32:06.690
- Well, maybe the investments,

00:32:06.690 --> 00:32:09.780
not every young man is pouring his money

00:32:09.780 --> 00:32:13.031
into investment properties
versus a Lamborghini.

00:32:13.031 --> 00:32:13.864
- [Tim] You also run your social media

00:32:13.864 --> 00:32:16.210
and obviously now taking control,

00:32:16.210 --> 00:32:18.342
so you may not call it leadership,

00:32:18.342 --> 00:32:22.367
but you're leading your
whim technically dropped it.

00:32:22.367 --> 00:32:25.760
- Well, the first time
I started to realize

00:32:25.760 --> 00:32:28.491
that I have to do something
with the money was,

00:32:28.491 --> 00:32:30.741
(laughter)

00:32:33.690 --> 00:32:37.813
when I moved to London when I
started to play for Reading,

00:32:39.370 --> 00:32:41.300
because you have to imagine,

00:32:41.300 --> 00:32:44.310
I have my friends and
everybody in Germany,

00:32:44.310 --> 00:32:46.693
and then I'm in Vegas, right?

00:32:47.557 --> 00:32:49.313
And then my agent calls
me and saying like,

00:32:49.313 --> 00:32:51.454
"Danny, I have an offer for you."

00:32:51.454 --> 00:32:53.115
And I was like, "Okay."

00:32:53.115 --> 00:32:55.677
And he goes, "Yeah, but
it's not in Germany,

00:32:55.677 --> 00:32:57.271
"It's like, you have to leave the country

00:32:57.271 --> 00:32:59.099
"and your parents and everyone."

00:32:59.099 --> 00:33:01.228
And I was like, "Cool, where is it?"

00:33:01.228 --> 00:33:04.007
And he goes, "It's a club called Reading."

00:33:04.007 --> 00:33:06.539
And I was like, "Where do I live?"

00:33:06.539 --> 00:33:08.170
And he goes, "You could live in London."

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.180
I was like, "Okay."

00:33:09.180 --> 00:33:11.710
And I went, I'm quite
a spontaneous person,

00:33:11.710 --> 00:33:15.860
and I think the people in
Germany, they're a bit uptight,

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:19.990
so my character and
the way I carry myself,

00:33:19.990 --> 00:33:23.127
I think it fits more
anyways to this city here.

00:33:23.127 --> 00:33:26.370
So I was like, you know
what, I'm open to do that.

00:33:26.370 --> 00:33:30.510
And so I rented a room in Reading,

00:33:30.510 --> 00:33:32.653
and I had nobody calling me,

00:33:33.547 --> 00:33:35.670
there was obviously my mom, my dad,

00:33:35.670 --> 00:33:37.980
they messaged me, whatever,
but I was just put on the spot,

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:41.840
no car, no bank account
set up yet, nothing.

00:33:41.840 --> 00:33:43.760
And I was thinking like, where are all--

00:33:43.760 --> 00:33:44.593
- How old were you?

00:33:44.593 --> 00:33:45.840
- I was 24.
- Okay.

00:33:45.840 --> 00:33:47.728
- I was like, where are all the people?

00:33:47.728 --> 00:33:50.028
(laughter)

00:33:50.028 --> 00:33:52.480
They don't see me on
TV anymore in Germany,

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:54.207
so I'm not running back no more,

00:33:54.207 --> 00:33:58.610
I still have to live and I
have to find, I don't know,

00:33:58.610 --> 00:34:00.023
people who are like--
- A social life.

00:34:00.023 --> 00:34:02.090
- A social life because
that's the most important,

00:34:02.090 --> 00:34:04.980
I always say if I'm not
happy, I don't perform well.

00:34:04.980 --> 00:34:09.160
And so then I moved to
London, and I started to--

00:34:09.160 --> 00:34:11.000
- Real quick, I apologize.
- Yeah.

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:13.210
- You say if I'm not happy,
I don't perform well.

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:16.640
I assume in the macro because
earlier, in the micro,

00:34:16.640 --> 00:34:18.230
you said if my parents are fighting

00:34:18.230 --> 00:34:20.370
before the match, I play better.

00:34:20.370 --> 00:34:23.150
Is that a macro micro thing,
or was that an evolution

00:34:23.150 --> 00:34:24.990
when you were younger,
that worked for you,

00:34:24.990 --> 00:34:28.240
but over time, as you grew up, it changed?

00:34:28.240 --> 00:34:30.740
- The thing is, obviously
the parents fighting

00:34:30.740 --> 00:34:33.060
was one thing, but I was still
happy about my social life.

00:34:33.060 --> 00:34:35.300
- Okay. (laughs)
- I was happy about my social.

00:34:35.300 --> 00:34:37.360
- So if your social life
is good, you play well.

00:34:37.360 --> 00:34:38.979
- I think so.
- I get it, I get it.

00:34:38.979 --> 00:34:40.077
Listen, I get it.
- If I'm happy

00:34:40.077 --> 00:34:41.238
from the inside--
- Yeah, I got it.

00:34:41.238 --> 00:34:42.491
- Then I was happy--
- I respect that.

00:34:42.491 --> 00:34:44.610
- And it shows on the
pitch, but when they fought,

00:34:44.610 --> 00:34:46.480
I can let the anger out as well--

00:34:46.480 --> 00:34:47.610
- I get it.
- So I could get--

00:34:47.610 --> 00:34:49.030
- That was the perfect of both worlds.

00:34:49.030 --> 00:34:49.863
- Yeah, exactly.

00:34:49.863 --> 00:34:51.420
- Your social life is killing it,

00:34:51.420 --> 00:34:53.790
your parents are fighting,
you fucking dominate.

00:34:53.790 --> 00:34:55.858
- Exactly.
- You got that, okay, got it.

00:34:55.858 --> 00:34:56.870
Just wanted to make sure.

00:34:56.870 --> 00:35:00.460
- No, but then I started
to hang out with people

00:35:00.460 --> 00:35:04.783
who would seem a bit more boring,

00:35:04.783 --> 00:35:06.045
(laughter)

00:35:06.045 --> 00:35:09.480
who would seem a bit more
boring to the type of friends

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.400
I used to have, but
actually more interesting

00:35:12.400 --> 00:35:13.233
because he pulled me and say like,

00:35:13.233 --> 00:35:16.807
"Danny, listen, you signed a
great contract with Reading.

00:35:16.807 --> 00:35:19.007
"Four years, that's
actually a big security,

00:35:19.007 --> 00:35:22.740
"and you have to start to
either buy something in London,"

00:35:22.740 --> 00:35:24.720
because the city is one of the leading

00:35:24.720 --> 00:35:27.490
cities in the world, it's
the capital, whatever.

00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:31.512
So the property market, I
think nine times out of 10,

00:35:31.512 --> 00:35:35.360
the price would go up and
you have to just generate

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:38.387
another income for you when
you're done playing football,

00:35:38.387 --> 00:35:41.610
and I said to him, "Okay,
I never heard about that."

00:35:41.610 --> 00:35:43.073
And it worked because
the fans I used to have,

00:35:43.073 --> 00:35:46.110
they just didn't advise
me on stuff like that.

00:35:46.110 --> 00:35:49.610
So I said, you know what,
I'm gonna rent a place

00:35:49.610 --> 00:35:53.320
in West London and see the
area and then after a year,

00:35:53.320 --> 00:35:58.060
I will decide where I wanna
invest, and I think that guy,

00:35:58.060 --> 00:36:00.440
he helped me a lot and I'll
be always grateful for that

00:36:00.440 --> 00:36:04.270
because looking now, I almost
paid all of the flats off,

00:36:04.270 --> 00:36:06.740
and I already generate income,

00:36:06.740 --> 00:36:09.970
and the places are
rented out, so I'm happy.

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:11.799
That's a good place, I would say.

00:36:11.799 --> 00:36:14.368
(laughter)

00:36:14.368 --> 00:36:15.623
- Yes, in the back.

00:36:16.777 --> 00:36:17.700
- [Audience Member] Hi, I
have a question for Gary.

00:36:17.700 --> 00:36:19.333
- Okay, I'll get you next.

00:36:21.630 --> 00:36:23.130
- [Audience Member] Is
there gonna be a flashback

00:36:23.130 --> 00:36:24.507
or a lashback of social media?

00:36:24.507 --> 00:36:26.410
So announcing on Facebook, Instagram,

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:29.454
we now see how long you're staying on it,

00:36:29.454 --> 00:36:32.621
are we moving into a cutback on those?

00:36:34.050 --> 00:36:36.360
- Yeah, the question was, will there be

00:36:36.360 --> 00:36:41.360
a pushback, a lash
against social time spent,

00:36:41.710 --> 00:36:43.060
the privacy issues,

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:45.410
I think it's already happened, right?

00:36:45.410 --> 00:36:47.410
You already see companies coming out,

00:36:47.410 --> 00:36:50.140
look at Apple, Apple is a company

00:36:50.140 --> 00:36:52.310
that's not winning in social, right?

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:54.170
There's apps on top of their app platform

00:36:54.170 --> 00:36:55.560
but they're not in it.

00:36:55.560 --> 00:36:57.480
And so if you're Apple, you're saying oh,

00:36:57.480 --> 00:37:00.500
if we build a default device on our phone

00:37:00.500 --> 00:37:02.710
that warns you of how much screen time,

00:37:02.710 --> 00:37:04.050
there might be a way for us to use that

00:37:04.050 --> 00:37:07.263
as a defensive play as an offense, right?

00:37:07.263 --> 00:37:10.940
I think the privacy thing
is a really interesting one,

00:37:10.940 --> 00:37:13.140
whether it's Brexit or
Trump or many other things

00:37:13.140 --> 00:37:15.650
that are going on, I
think we as human beings

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:18.090
don't like to hold ourselves accountable.

00:37:18.090 --> 00:37:21.840
And so I do think there'll
be a backlash against social,

00:37:21.840 --> 00:37:23.750
but I think it's gonna be more of us

00:37:23.750 --> 00:37:26.270
passing on responsibility of things

00:37:26.270 --> 00:37:29.040
that we don't want to see in ourselves.

00:37:29.040 --> 00:37:31.920
I think parents are very
good at judging millennials

00:37:31.920 --> 00:37:34.530
without realizing they're
the ones who raised them

00:37:34.530 --> 00:37:36.300
and created the entitlement.

00:37:36.300 --> 00:37:39.820
So I think the human condition speaks to,

00:37:39.820 --> 00:37:42.520
I believe the people that
tend to win the most in life

00:37:42.520 --> 00:37:45.670
are the ones that are the most
willing to be accountable.

00:37:45.670 --> 00:37:48.740
And I believe that the masses
do not want to be accountable.

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:51.004
And I do believe that the mainstream media

00:37:51.004 --> 00:37:55.740
has a lot of financial reasons
to downplay the digital world

00:37:55.740 --> 00:37:57.440
because it's taking their money,

00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:00.093
and I think that all these
dynamics are playing out.

00:38:01.136 --> 00:38:02.490
So I think there will be a backlash.

00:38:02.490 --> 00:38:06.000
Now, I think it'll be
a blip on the screen,

00:38:06.000 --> 00:38:08.670
I think that we are going towards,

00:38:08.670 --> 00:38:10.497
I mean, when my friends are like,

00:38:10.497 --> 00:38:14.270
"Oh, I'm limiting my son's screen time,"

00:38:14.270 --> 00:38:16.427
I'm like, "You do know what world your son

00:38:16.427 --> 00:38:18.470
"will live in in 15 years, right?"

00:38:18.470 --> 00:38:20.090
Just wanna make sure everybody knows,

00:38:20.090 --> 00:38:21.823
we won't be going backwards.

00:38:22.661 --> 00:38:26.190
Wait till voices mature,
and you're talking to Alexa

00:38:26.190 --> 00:38:27.940
like it's your friend.

00:38:27.940 --> 00:38:31.554
Wait to AR and VR and all
these things continue,

00:38:31.554 --> 00:38:34.090
the robots are gonna kill us in the end,

00:38:35.110 --> 00:38:40.110
and so to me, it'll be a micro blip,

00:38:40.201 --> 00:38:45.201
three years, six years, six
months, but I think (laughs)

00:38:46.270 --> 00:38:49.290
when the whole Facebook Cambridge
Analytica thing happened,

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:53.653
people were posting protests
of Facebook on Instagram.

00:38:56.110 --> 00:38:58.300
It was just the most fun
thing for me to watch,

00:38:58.300 --> 00:39:01.580
the human condi-- like fuck you, Facebook,

00:39:01.580 --> 00:39:05.127
on Instagram, watching those.

00:39:05.127 --> 00:39:09.060
And so I think that, I think we will,

00:39:09.060 --> 00:39:11.210
but I don't think it means anything

00:39:11.210 --> 00:39:14.595
in the inevitable macro
of technology advancement.

00:39:14.595 --> 00:39:15.428
- [Audience Member] Thank you.

00:39:15.428 --> 00:39:16.772
- Technology always wins.

00:39:16.772 --> 00:39:18.065
- [Audience Member] True.

00:39:18.065 --> 00:39:23.020
- Because we value
convenience over everything.

00:39:23.020 --> 00:39:26.850
We are animals that value
time, subconsciously,

00:39:26.850 --> 00:39:30.416
and we will give up
everything, including money,

00:39:30.416 --> 00:39:32.820
and that's what we spend
our money on, by the way,

00:39:32.820 --> 00:39:34.090
if you play it out so.

00:39:34.090 --> 00:39:35.666
Yeah, sir, yeah.

00:39:35.666 --> 00:39:37.332
- [Audience Member]
Yeah, question for Danny,

00:39:37.332 --> 00:39:38.970
you spent time with both
the German and the US

00:39:38.970 --> 00:39:41.520
national team, what makes
the biggest difference

00:39:41.520 --> 00:39:44.897
in the national teams,
and then who do you want

00:39:44.897 --> 00:39:47.632
to be the next US national team coach?

00:39:47.632 --> 00:39:48.465
- Okay--
- The question,

00:39:48.465 --> 00:39:49.830
just for everybody watching on camera,

00:39:49.830 --> 00:39:52.300
difference between the German
and American national team,

00:39:52.300 --> 00:39:55.247
and who do you want the
next American coach to be

00:39:55.247 --> 00:39:57.380
on the American national team?

00:39:57.380 --> 00:39:59.260
- I think the biggest difference

00:39:59.260 --> 00:40:03.625
between the German national
team and the US national team--

00:40:03.625 --> 00:40:04.904
- And help me, just for me--
- And football.

00:40:04.904 --> 00:40:05.737
- Real quick--
- Yeah.

00:40:05.737 --> 00:40:07.169
- How old were you when you
were on the German team,

00:40:07.169 --> 00:40:09.890
and how old were you on the American team?

00:40:09.890 --> 00:40:12.740
- I played Germany until I was 17,

00:40:14.534 --> 00:40:15.951
I'd say 17 or 18.

00:40:16.785 --> 00:40:21.750
And then I started to play
for the USA when I was 21.

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:23.284
- Okay, keep going.
- Yeah.

00:40:23.284 --> 00:40:25.630
So the biggest difference is definitely

00:40:26.580 --> 00:40:31.060
the education in tactically, technically,

00:40:31.060 --> 00:40:33.880
because if I look at this, US,

00:40:33.880 --> 00:40:37.420
the country is so much
bigger than Germany, right?

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:42.420
And I think the US has amazing athletes,

00:40:43.080 --> 00:40:46.840
but they just don't know
how to use their speed,

00:40:46.840 --> 00:40:48.280
their power, their strength,

00:40:48.280 --> 00:40:50.420
because I would say we
have better athletes

00:40:50.420 --> 00:40:53.891
than the Germans, in general,
just like you can see

00:40:53.891 --> 00:40:57.990
sprinters and whatever, there's
never a German guy winning,

00:40:57.990 --> 00:41:02.990
but they focus so much on
technically and tactical work

00:41:06.110 --> 00:41:08.790
that actually invested a
lot of money and research

00:41:08.790 --> 00:41:11.060
there as well because the youth cultures,

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:13.710
they then fly to Barcelona,
they go to Amsterdam,

00:41:13.710 --> 00:41:16.330
where all the amazing
academies are, and they learn

00:41:16.330 --> 00:41:18.300
from them, whereas in the States, I think

00:41:18.300 --> 00:41:21.580
because it's not the number
one sport of the country,

00:41:21.580 --> 00:41:24.690
they're not really interested
in that, and they don't,

00:41:24.690 --> 00:41:28.600
yeah, they don't like to be
bad in something, which now,

00:41:28.600 --> 00:41:32.102
since we didn't make the
work up, changed a lot.

00:41:32.102 --> 00:41:33.484
I think there's a lot of more

00:41:33.484 --> 00:41:35.953
investment going on for American soccer.

00:41:37.503 --> 00:41:38.490
- And what about coach?

00:41:38.490 --> 00:41:43.490
- Coach, so I think we
have the interims coach

00:41:44.020 --> 00:41:47.150
at the moment, Dave Sarachan,
and he's doing an amazing job,

00:41:47.150 --> 00:41:49.996
I have to say, I mean
we drew against France,

00:41:49.996 --> 00:41:53.350
just a month before they
won the World Cup, right?

00:41:53.350 --> 00:41:58.350
And he definitely changed
the strategy of players,

00:41:58.620 --> 00:42:01.320
scouting and recruitment, and he focuses

00:42:01.320 --> 00:42:03.860
a lot more on the younger players.

00:42:03.860 --> 00:42:07.283
And in the past, you had a
lot of politics going on,

00:42:08.600 --> 00:42:11.680
for whatever reasons,
which I got a bit slack off

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:13.450
because I came out and said it.

00:42:13.450 --> 00:42:16.985
But yeah, there were players who already--

00:42:16.985 --> 00:42:18.370
- Past their prime.
- Past their prime.

00:42:18.370 --> 00:42:21.070
Like Germany now, you see
it because they wonder

00:42:21.070 --> 00:42:24.050
where I come four years
ago, and people thought

00:42:24.050 --> 00:42:26.310
that some players, certain players,

00:42:26.310 --> 00:42:30.450
they'll have to start every
game, and that was wrong.

00:42:30.450 --> 00:42:31.890
And that happened because with the States,

00:42:31.890 --> 00:42:34.030
and it cost us the World Cup.

00:42:34.030 --> 00:42:38.580
So I just hope that the
next manager will be someone

00:42:38.580 --> 00:42:43.003
who focuses a lot on the
youth and makes decisions

00:42:43.003 --> 00:42:46.220
football based, and not political based.

00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:48.470
- I think it's, from
somebody from a very outside

00:42:48.470 --> 00:42:50.173
point of view in the sport,

00:42:51.820 --> 00:42:53.940
but who watches culture very carefully,

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.470
I think the most interesting
thing about US football

00:42:56.470 --> 00:42:58.610
in the long term is two core things,

00:42:58.610 --> 00:43:02.640
one, it's just the popularity
of the sport is real,

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:05.840
if you spend time in
America, 15 years ago,

00:43:05.840 --> 00:43:10.840
I never saw a single kid in
America wear a soccer jersey,

00:43:11.560 --> 00:43:13.620
it never happened.

00:43:13.620 --> 00:43:18.620
Now you see as many kids, six
to 12 wearing football jerseys

00:43:20.370 --> 00:43:23.080
as you do see them wear
baseball, outside of

00:43:23.080 --> 00:43:24.510
basketball or football
are still number one

00:43:24.510 --> 00:43:27.640
but baseball and hockey
and proper football

00:43:27.640 --> 00:43:30.370
are now hovering around
the same popularity.

00:43:30.370 --> 00:43:33.760
Number two, the Latino
population in the US

00:43:33.760 --> 00:43:37.530
is so extreme and exploding
that I think the last names

00:43:37.530 --> 00:43:40.110
will look more like Brazil
or Mexico or things of that

00:43:40.110 --> 00:43:45.110
nature in 16 or 20 years,
but the resources are there.

00:43:45.110 --> 00:43:49.610
It's not religion, like
Lebron's playing basketball,

00:43:49.610 --> 00:43:52.870
not defense, and so I think
that's gonna be interesting

00:43:52.870 --> 00:43:54.220
to watch over the next 20 years.

00:43:54.220 --> 00:43:56.090
Alright, one more question.

00:43:56.090 --> 00:43:57.113
- [Audience Member] Hey, so I've known you

00:43:57.113 --> 00:43:59.600
over the last 10 years
that you've been super,

00:43:59.600 --> 00:44:01.750
one of your cues for culture is hip hop.

00:44:01.750 --> 00:44:02.583
- Yes.

00:44:02.583 --> 00:44:03.510
- [Audience Member] From
our first interview,

00:44:03.510 --> 00:44:05.800
you've always entered the big artists

00:44:05.800 --> 00:44:10.173
but more recently, you featured
Game and Nipsy and 8boogie.

00:44:11.200 --> 00:44:12.960
As you got your nuances
from culture when it comes

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:15.440
to building a business
in America and the UK,

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:18.420
will you align yourself with UK rappers

00:44:18.420 --> 00:44:19.770
as a source of lending the culture,

00:44:19.770 --> 00:44:22.400
like Gigs and Skepta, Big Shot Jamie,

00:44:22.400 --> 00:44:24.863
and my question is, what
nuances will you be looking for

00:44:24.863 --> 00:44:27.960
when you talk to those
artists to teach you

00:44:27.960 --> 00:44:29.300
things about things here in the UK?

00:44:29.300 --> 00:44:31.710
- Yeah, so what my dear friend here

00:44:31.710 --> 00:44:32.670
who's known me for a long time

00:44:32.670 --> 00:44:34.560
knows I've always really loved hip hop,

00:44:34.560 --> 00:44:38.043
as my Instagram over the
last two years has exploded,

00:44:38.990 --> 00:44:42.530
in parallel, Mike Boyd, who's
always been close to me,

00:44:42.530 --> 00:44:44.650
was freed up to really do strategy for me

00:44:44.650 --> 00:44:47.984
and we took approach about 18
months ago to get very serious

00:44:47.984 --> 00:44:51.430
about the emerging hip hop
artists that we thought,

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:53.420
in a five to seven year window,

00:44:53.420 --> 00:44:56.010
could become the Drakes or Lil Waynes,

00:44:56.010 --> 00:44:56.843
and what would that mean

00:44:56.843 --> 00:44:59.170
and getting in the trenches of culture.

00:44:59.170 --> 00:45:01.630
The question is, am I thinking about that

00:45:01.630 --> 00:45:04.480
in a UK lens as well?

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:05.850
And it's kind of interesting

00:45:05.850 --> 00:45:06.937
because maybe you've noticed this,

00:45:06.937 --> 00:45:08.950
and it's probably why, not probably,

00:45:08.950 --> 00:45:11.240
it's why we're sitting here right now.

00:45:11.240 --> 00:45:14.060
I've taken the approach in the UK,

00:45:14.060 --> 00:45:15.970
and we're opening up
Singapore in February,

00:45:15.970 --> 00:45:17.330
we're gonna open up Brazil,

00:45:17.330 --> 00:45:19.970
it looks like now in 2019 as well.

00:45:19.970 --> 00:45:22.370
It's interesting, my
ambition to get deeper

00:45:22.370 --> 00:45:26.720
into proper football,
having a son and daughter

00:45:26.720 --> 00:45:29.000
that I want to have a
team and go through it,

00:45:29.000 --> 00:45:32.437
knowing that I'm gonna be
here six or 10 times a year,

00:45:32.437 --> 00:45:35.270
and because I think authenticity matters,

00:45:35.270 --> 00:45:38.368
because I've historically
not gravitated to the artists

00:45:38.368 --> 00:45:40.860
that you just mentioned,
I've actually taken

00:45:40.860 --> 00:45:44.430
the macro thesis of what
emerging hip hop artists

00:45:44.430 --> 00:45:46.920
do for me in a pulse of culture.

00:45:46.920 --> 00:45:51.890
In the US, I think the
culture of Premier League

00:45:51.890 --> 00:45:55.520
and proper football culture
in the UK and Europe

00:45:55.520 --> 00:45:58.310
as a whole is the way
I'm thinking about that.

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.930
I'm trying to spend time and
listen and pay attention,

00:46:00.930 --> 00:46:03.380
I spend an enormous
amount of time looking,

00:46:03.380 --> 00:46:08.120
in the US, I may be World
Star as an Instagram account,

00:46:08.120 --> 00:46:11.130
but for the way I'm thinking
about Europe and the UK

00:46:11.130 --> 00:46:14.070
in general, it'll be 433 that I'll spend

00:46:14.070 --> 00:46:15.190
more time looking at.

00:46:15.190 --> 00:46:18.090
And so interestingly,
especially given that

00:46:18.090 --> 00:46:21.350
we're sitting here together,
the approach is not

00:46:21.350 --> 00:46:24.520
going to be emerging hip
hop artists in the UK,

00:46:24.520 --> 00:46:27.210
unless just like everything in my life,

00:46:27.210 --> 00:46:30.570
one artist catches my
imagination at the right time,

00:46:30.570 --> 00:46:33.990
you know this, almost everything
you love, food, sports,

00:46:33.990 --> 00:46:37.341
whatever it may be, it
was one thing that then,

00:46:37.341 --> 00:46:39.610
that you fell in love with
and were curious about

00:46:39.610 --> 00:46:41.150
that got you deep into the rabbit hole.

00:46:41.150 --> 00:46:44.880
So I wouldn't say never, I
allow for a lot of serendipity,

00:46:44.880 --> 00:46:47.340
but I also am thoughtful
and have a lot of strategy,

00:46:47.340 --> 00:46:50.580
I think the culture of
football here is what

00:46:50.580 --> 00:46:53.660
I'm spending a lot of
time on, both selfishly

00:46:53.660 --> 00:46:55.190
'cause I wanna suck myself into it,

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:56.660
'cause I wanna get into it.

00:46:56.660 --> 00:46:59.050
And because I think it is an incredible

00:46:59.050 --> 00:47:01.433
important currency of the culture.

00:47:02.410 --> 00:47:03.530
Mary, I saw you raise your hand,

00:47:03.530 --> 00:47:05.290
so you're gonna sneak it in.

00:47:05.290 --> 00:47:07.620
- [Mary] Yeah, sorry, so
just a question for you,

00:47:07.620 --> 00:47:10.639
how can brands drive true
authenticity with influencers

00:47:10.639 --> 00:47:12.540
while still retaining commerciality?

00:47:12.540 --> 00:47:15.160
So how do we make money
through influencers

00:47:15.160 --> 00:47:16.860
without putting our consumers off?

00:47:18.160 --> 00:47:21.240
- By giving the influencer 100% autonomy

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:24.470
and eliminating our egos
as brands and thinking that

00:47:24.470 --> 00:47:27.470
we know exactly what it
stands for and what it means.

00:47:27.470 --> 00:47:29.760
I think what the biggest eye opener to me

00:47:29.760 --> 00:47:34.190
in corporate America and
brand world is the level

00:47:34.190 --> 00:47:38.223
of ideology and audacity and
ego, like the thought that,

00:47:39.610 --> 00:47:43.050
for all the normal people
here, Coca Cola and Heineken

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:45.730
and Adidas think that the brand means

00:47:45.730 --> 00:47:47.990
the same thing to all of you.

00:47:47.990 --> 00:47:49.110
And that's just laughable.

00:47:49.110 --> 00:47:53.680
Everybody here, when I say
the word Supreme or Kentucky

00:47:53.680 --> 00:47:55.470
Fried Chicken, are
thinking different things,

00:47:55.470 --> 00:47:57.610
disgusting, awesome, rad,
I don't know what that is.

00:47:57.610 --> 00:48:02.610
But and so there's an ideology
that brand managers and CMOs

00:48:03.690 --> 00:48:06.290
in corporate America have about
what their brand stands for.

00:48:06.290 --> 00:48:07.840
If you're gonna do influencer marketing,

00:48:07.840 --> 00:48:09.790
what you need to do is you need to

00:48:09.790 --> 00:48:14.020
buy sponsorship through
influencers at a good deal.

00:48:14.020 --> 00:48:15.860
They know their audience.

00:48:15.860 --> 00:48:19.090
He, if he decided he wanted
to do that, I, any of you,

00:48:19.090 --> 00:48:21.900
you know your audience, and
so if your interpretation

00:48:21.900 --> 00:48:26.800
of this chocolate shake or
sneaker or blow dryer is,

00:48:28.090 --> 00:48:30.350
it needs to be funny and snarky,

00:48:30.350 --> 00:48:32.560
that's exactly as a brand what you want.

00:48:32.560 --> 00:48:35.720
You're giving him, me,
somebody else, money

00:48:35.720 --> 00:48:38.430
so that you have something
happen for your business.

00:48:38.430 --> 00:48:41.900
Stella wants business, the
best thing for Stella to do

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:45.560
is get out of the way, they,
much like the small business

00:48:45.560 --> 00:48:47.720
question, they're in charge of picking it,

00:48:47.720 --> 00:48:49.556
you picked the influencer.

00:48:49.556 --> 00:48:53.600
You decided that influencer is
somebody I want because she's

00:48:53.600 --> 00:48:57.440
so fucking awesome, I want to
give her money so I sell shit.

00:48:57.440 --> 00:49:00.870
Why are you micromanaging
her and telling her,

00:49:00.870 --> 00:49:02.560
you better use the word great?

00:49:02.560 --> 00:49:04.350
I've never used the word, I've never done

00:49:04.350 --> 00:49:07.628
a sponsorship deal in my life,
and I have big audiences.

00:49:07.628 --> 00:49:09.170
There's a couple of reasons.

00:49:09.170 --> 00:49:11.970
It's not how I think about my
brand, and it's not what I do.

00:49:11.970 --> 00:49:13.640
But even like the K-Swiss thing,

00:49:13.640 --> 00:49:15.810
that's a partnership, right?

00:49:15.810 --> 00:49:18.350
But when I signed my K-Swiss deal,

00:49:18.350 --> 00:49:19.920
the most interesting
part of the negotiation

00:49:19.920 --> 00:49:23.700
was the standard deal, do
you have a sneaker deal?

00:49:23.700 --> 00:49:24.533
- Puma.

00:49:24.533 --> 00:49:26.690
- Great, so the standard deal is like,

00:49:26.690 --> 00:49:28.840
motherfucker, you don't
wear anything else.

00:49:29.870 --> 00:49:33.540
I literally at the 11th hour
walked from the K-Swiss deal

00:49:33.540 --> 00:49:35.890
because I wanted to wear
whatever I wanted to wear.

00:49:35.890 --> 00:49:38.010
If I wanted to wear Air Force
1s, which I like to wear

00:49:38.010 --> 00:49:41.200
quite a bit, I didn't want, I
needed the authenticity of me,

00:49:41.200 --> 00:49:42.780
I didn't need the economics enough.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:46.470
And I think that's the best
influencer, not the worst.

00:49:46.470 --> 00:49:50.272
Because we smell out bullshit,
we're very, very good at it.

00:49:50.272 --> 00:49:52.740
You can trick us for a minute,

00:49:52.740 --> 00:49:55.110
but not for an hour and
definitely not for a year.

00:49:55.110 --> 00:49:59.940
And so I think that brands
need to check their egos

00:49:59.940 --> 00:50:01.170
and get out of the way.

00:50:01.170 --> 00:50:02.340
Much like I told that young man,

00:50:02.340 --> 00:50:03.240
you're in control.

00:50:03.240 --> 00:50:05.700
You can say no, I don't wanna
work with you anymore, right?

00:50:05.700 --> 00:50:07.650
Brands don't have to pick the influencer,

00:50:07.650 --> 00:50:09.443
but once they pick it, they
need to get out of the way

00:50:09.443 --> 00:50:11.610
and let the influencer do their thing.

00:50:11.610 --> 00:50:14.213
That's the reason you picked
them in the first place.

00:50:15.160 --> 00:50:16.130
Thank you.

00:50:16.130 --> 00:50:17.590
Thanks everyone for being here.

00:50:17.590 --> 00:50:19.591
Let's clap it up for this wonderful man.

00:50:19.591 --> 00:50:21.841
(clapping)

00:50:24.880 --> 00:50:28.100
You keep asking questions,
we'll keep answering them.

00:50:28.990 --> 00:50:30.427
Awesome, thank you, guys.

00:50:30.427 --> 00:50:34.177
(hip hop instrumental music)

